WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:18.720
- Hi everyone, welcome to the
Daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.620
We have a exciting, I guess
we could call it a show today.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.910
Just to get everyone on the same page

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.360
of what this thing even is.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.700
If you're showing up for the first time

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.760
we're doing these live streams
as a way to stay connected

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.010
during school closures.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.810
When we started seeing the school closures

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.280
and now seems a lifetime ago,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.240
but it was I guess it was
four or five weeks ago,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.060
we realized that all of us at Khan Academy

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.270
with as a non-for-profit
with a mission of providing

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.050
a free world-class education
for anyone anywhere

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.740
that we had to step up
and we had to figure out

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.080
how to support all of you as parents,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.460
as teachers and students
as much as possible.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.710
So we started publishing
things like daily schedules

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.490
to structure all of the
content on Khan Academy,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.830
learning plans, teacher-parent webinars

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:56.940
and also this homeroom

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.780
and it's a place to talk about everything.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.050
The closures themselves,
the economy, COVID virus.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.530
Many times just ask
any questions you have.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.290
We have an awesome guest
today, I encourage you,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.460
it's going to be Mellody Hobson.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.000
I'll give a better introduction
for her in a few seconds.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.150
But feel free and start asking questions

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:13.983
on the message boards

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.820
either for myself or
for Mellody especially.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.370
I wanna give everyone a
reminder, we are not-for-profit.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.440
We are funded primarily through
philanthropic donations.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.450
And so if you are in a position to do so,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.710
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:30.100
We were already running at a
deficit before school closures

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.130
and now our traffic is almost three X

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:32.990
of what it typically is.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.710
And you could imagine
we're trying to do more

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.543
in this context.

00:01:35.543 --> 00:01:37.910
So, for us to be able
to do the work for folks

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.930
who might not be able to
afford, anything is appreciated.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.870
I do wanna give a special shoutout

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.520
to several corporations who
stepped up in the last few weeks

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.450
to really especially
around the COVID response

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.643
Bank of America, Fastly,
Novartis, Google.org, and AT&amp;T.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.790
As with that, I'm eager
to have as much time

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.480
with our guests as possible.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.730
I've known our guests
Mellody for some time,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.690
Mellody Hobson is the co-CEO

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.870
and President of Ariel investments.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.970
And, there's two things I really I'm eager

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.730
to talk to you about Mellody.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.620
One is just your journey
to becoming co-CEO

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.390
of what I think is one
of the most important

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.700
investment firms in the world.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.990
And your view of the economy,

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:22.490
we brought it, had a couple of folks

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:23.990
on in the investment management world

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.660
and it's just interesting
times to put it lightly.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.260
So maybe a good place to start Mellody,

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.100
tell us there's a lot
of young people watching

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.550
who are trying to figure out their life,

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.250
what should they do with their life?

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.530
Tell us a little bit about your journey.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.530
How did you end up becoming the
co-CEO of Ariel investments?

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.730
- Well, thank you so much
for having me on Sal.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.170
And just know that I'm
such a big fan of yours,

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.750
as you know, all too
well, from the first time

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.640
I came to see you I told
you I was just enamored

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.400
by all the things you were
doing to teach so many people

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.750
all around the world and
I applaud your efforts

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.160
and just feel so much gratitude

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.880
that you have things like this that exist

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.440
for people all over the world.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.880
I'm also honored to be asked
to be one of the people

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.800
that you interview and to tell my story.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.880
My story I think is the
story of many people

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.540
around the world in so many ways,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.530
just a story of persistence
and really, really hard work.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.010
I grew up in Chicago,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.470
and I was the youngest of six children.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.280
My mom was a single mom

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.890
and when I was growing up,

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:32.090
we had a really at times
challenging existence,

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.310
to say the least sometimes we got evicted,

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:38.360
sometimes our phone got
disconnected, lights got cut off.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.530
We'd live in some of
the abandoned apartments

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.160
that my mom used to try to fix up

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.600
and rent for people in
order to make a living.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.980
And it is during that
time that I became clear

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.660
that I was desperate to understand money,

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.580
not make it, not have
a lot, understand it.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.780
I thought if I could understand money,

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.010
I could have a better life

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.830
and I really committed myself to that.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.330
When I was 19 years old,

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.500
I had the opportunity to be an
intern at Ariel investments,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.280
which was this fledgling investment firm,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.650
started by this wondercan
named John Rogers,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.530
who started the company
when he was 24 years old.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.310
Today, we're 37 years old now.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.210
And after I graduated from college,

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:19.590
I went back and worked
at the firm in 1991,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.510
after graduating from Princeton,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.560
and I've only had one job since then.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.390
And I started off on the client
services and marketing side

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.850
learning everything I could
learned about investing

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.560
in the stock market, and over time rose up

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.220
in the organization.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.530
In May of 2000, I became president,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.130
I went from intern to president.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.310
And then last summer in
2019, I became co-CEO.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.020
It's an amazing story in so many ways,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.130
and I have so much gratitude
for the opportunity

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.070
I've been given, but most of all,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.870
I have the most amount of gratitude

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.730
for what I've been able
to learn about money

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.230
and investing over this
near 30-year career

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.600
that I've been doing this

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.280
and how that is really put in me a fire

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.270
to teach what I know to other people

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.070
so that they can have a better life.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.180
- And what's your sense?

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.000
What do you think made your
trajectory so successful?

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.060
What do you think were the traits

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.120
that made you a successful investor

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.570
and allowed other people
to recognize it as well?

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.750
- Well, I think the first
thing that helped me

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.810
was that I always believe

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.830
that you have to always
be learning, always,

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.730
that I ever have felt even today

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.510
that I know enough and then I can stop.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.290
And so having that open mind,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.050
some people call it a beginner's mind.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.440
That's a Buddhist concept
that your mind is always new,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.810
like a child, and you're
approaching everything

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.780
from the perspective of a beginner,

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.260
allows you to really take
in a lot of information.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.100
Ray Kroc who started
McDonald's had a quote

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:49.933
that I really liked.

00:05:49.933 --> 00:05:53.347
Ray says, "As long as
you're green, you grow,

00:05:53.347 --> 00:05:55.450
"when you're ripe you rot."

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.320
And I really liked that concept

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.590
of just staying green all
the time and being open.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.040
Not being so convinced
that you know everything,

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:04.640
I think that made a difference.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.470
Secondly, a commitment to learning.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.940
We call ourselves a
learning culture at Ariel.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.050
We always wanna build on
what we learned from before.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.550
So right now being in
this global pandemic,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.430
we're building on all
the knowledge we learned

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.660
in the financial crisis of 2008 and nine.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.870
And then during that period,
we built on the things

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.670
that we learned in 9/11.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:27.040
And that we built on things we
learned in the crash of '87.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.530
So it's like constantly
accumulating knowledge

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.140
that will make you better,
and building on it,

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.430
till you have more and more muscle memory.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.890
And the last thing is just
I love the idea of learning

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.550
about the markets,
talking about companies,

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.550
really understanding how
money works around the world.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.410
It just really fascinates me.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:47.840
And so I think it's very important,

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.600
you become more successful

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.850
at something that you really like.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:55.090
And I just really love
the business that I'm in.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:56.160
And at the end of the day,

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.250
I tell our team that we work with,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.990
we get to make people's lives better

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.070
because we grow their
money in their pension fund

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.580
or their kids college education money,

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.410
that's on our mutual funds,
whatever it might be,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.030
so that they can have a better outcome.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:13.630
And that's as fulfilling as
anything that anyone can do

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:15.820
as fulfilling as you teaching maybe,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.780
as fulfilling as doctors
who are doing what they do.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.263
We feel that our work is that important.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.053
- And we're getting a
lot of questions often

00:07:24.053 --> 00:07:26.790
from YouTube and social
media which is great.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.190
For Aditya Gupta.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:30.170
And this is a good starter question.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.980
A lot of people are
kinda vaguely familiar,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.180
okay, they're these things,
these investment funds.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.430
But his question is, what
does Ariel investments do?

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.190
And maybe I'll extend his question,

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.260
What is your day like?

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.930
And maybe what is it
like when you started?

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.330
What does an investment
manager and analysts do?

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.430
- So let's start off with
Ariel investments does.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:54.430
We are a boutique, independent,
money management company,

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:58.300
we manage stock portfolios
for big institutions,

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:03.300
so think states, cities, foundations,

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.990
whole host of organizations, often pension

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.340
but it could be endowment money as well.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:10.730
And then for individuals,

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.120
we have publicly traded mutual funds

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.330
that anyone can invest with us,

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:19.530
who has as little as $50 a month
to put in our mutual funds.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.260
We buy domestic companies,

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.670
US companies that are small
and medium sized companies

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.290
that are out of favor

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.510
but who are strong potential for growth.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.960
And over the years, we've
owned everything from companies

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.910
that you know, they
usually dominate the niche

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:33.960
that they're in.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.630
Everything from Smucker's jelly,

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.260
which is in the portfolio right now.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.490
In the past, we've owned things

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.500
like Tiffany's, The Retailer and Clorox

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.200
the diversified consumer products company.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.070
So you name it, those
companies that dominated niche.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.030
We say once you get the customer,

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.850
they go back again and again,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.650
small and medium-sized companies.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:58.490
And then we have international
and global portfolios.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:01.630
International means it's
only companies based outside

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.210
of the United States

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.950
and global means it's
companies all over the world,

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.200
including the United States.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.280
And those portfolios also
seek to invest in companies

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.590
that are out of favor.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:17.000
And that's everything
from higher companies

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.000
to the version of what
you would have in America

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.740
with some of our internet businesses

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.410
that are based in China, like Baidu.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.770
So, we're looking for these
brands and franchises.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.040
And we want businesses that
will be able to grow over time,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.280
but we wanna buy them
when they're out of favor,

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.300
when for whatever reason
they've been cast aside.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.630
So as you might imagine,
right now, during a time

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:43.210
when the stock market
has been so volatile,

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.163
there have been a lot
of buying opportunities

00:09:45.163 --> 00:09:46.760
that have come to us.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.890
So these days, we're super busy.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:51.360
But when you ask about my day,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.940
I am actually not an investment analyst,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.000
although I am someone whose Rolodex

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.650
is used on a regular
basis to inform the stocks

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:02.270
that we are buying.

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:03.920
So in this current environment,

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.570
I've been on the phone on many occasions

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.330
with my co-CEO calling
friends in the industry,

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.360
talking about what they're
seeing in the market

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.860
because we can learn from everyone,

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:18.310
including we called his old
corporate finance teacher,

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:19.590
someone named Burton Malkiel,

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.960
who wrote a book called "The
Random Walk Down Wall Street."

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:25.160
that is so famous, I think
it's in its ninth edition,

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.500
to say, what do you think
of this environment?

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.220
But we'll also call very
famous money managers

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:31.403
and ask them that as well.

00:10:31.403 --> 00:10:33.540
When we do our own independent work

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.240
and our own independent thinking,

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.250
but we can learn from anyone.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.010
And then our team would
be spending lots of time

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.580
on the phone, talking to
management teams of companies

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.300
that we own, asking them
what their prospects

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.880
for the business are, what
has changed, what is the same,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.700
how are they managing during the period?

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.300
And most important of all,
do they have the liquidity

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.540
to get them to the other side?

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:01.680
And I say the other side
because this will end

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.330
at some point, it won't be forever.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:06.470
At some point there will be a vaccine.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.940
At some point we will go
back to life as we know it,

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:11.600
we don't know how long that will be.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.920
And so the most important
thing is to understand

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.320
from those companies, that
they have the balance sheet

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.460
and the wherewithal to
be able to withstand

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.990
whatever kind of pain they
will feel during this period.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:27.950
I spend an enormous amount
of time on the phone talking

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.430
to our clients and
shareholders, writing to them,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.250
writing the communications
explaining what we're doing,

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.210
so that they're as informed as possible

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:39.120
and there are no surprises
when it comes to our portfolios

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.070
and how we're investing and performing.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.320
- And touching on I
think a lot of questions

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.890
are which are, where's the economy going?

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:49.291
Before I go into that
there's something you said

00:11:49.291 --> 00:11:50.360
that is interesting, I
think a lot of people

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:51.570
could learn from.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.270
The fact that you're looking at companies

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:55.300
that are out of favor,

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.970
that might be counterintuitive for some.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:59.550
So what's the hypothesis there

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.260
and also I'm fascinated by
your logo, which is a turtle.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:04.773
So, and maybe those two
things are connected.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:08.630
- Well, we believe that real
money is made over time.

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:11.920
And all of our beliefs come from studying

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.453
the investment grades.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:18.240
The greatest investor of our
modern time is Warren Buffett,

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.840
the CEO and chairman
of Berkshire Hathaway,

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:22.380
who still happens to be alive,

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.220
who is one of the greatest
track records in investing

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:27.190
of any human being ever.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.590
And so we've thought a lot
about what he's written

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:32.590
about and talked about,
and he talks about time

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:36.490
is the friend of the wonderful investor,

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:38.450
the enemy of the mediocre.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.520
And so we think about that fact

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.270
that we wanna invest in
things that over the long term

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.400
will demonstrate growth.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.580
And there may be a reason
that it's temporary,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.160
out of favor, maybe an entire industry

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.750
got taken down at once

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.020
and a good company went down with it.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:57.240
And so we're looking
for those opportunities

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.930
to be able to buy companies
when for whatever reason

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.560
something has gone wrong.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.290
So a simple example of that

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.590
we've been buying a company recently

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:08.010
that's in the dental industry.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.140
And that's very much of late.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.120
And the reason that it's compelling

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.360
is because we say to ourselves, okay,

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.630
we get it when you're sheltered in place,

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.830
no one can go to the dentist.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:21.010
So the earnings and
revenue on that business

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.570
just fall off a cliff.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:24.490
There's no question about it.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.120
Then our next question is,

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.370
can they manage through this period

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:30.730
for a significant period
of time not knowing

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.070
when it'll be over.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.720
They're best in class at what they do.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.430
And when things get up and running again,

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.640
we believe most people will
go back to the dentist.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:43.420
In fact, they'll probably be
some pent up demand there.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.950
So that would be an
example of out of favor,

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.360
but shows potential for long-term growth.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.020
When you think of around the world,

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.090
more and more people go to the dentist.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:53.760
I'll give you another quick example.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.300
Medical related, we own
a company called Zimmer

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:57.920
that makes hips and knees.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.240
And they literally, it's
a doctor gets trained

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.190
on one device, and they use
that device all the time.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.510
So it's in our mind a
razor, razor blade business.

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:11.000
The doctor is the razor, the
customer is the razor blade.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:12.990
The customer comes through
and gets hips and knees

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:14.470
over and over again.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:17.910
Again, during this pandemic,
no elective surgery,

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:21.160
having a bad hip or knee doesn't kill you,

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.770
but it might really hurt.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.270
So people can't go to the doctor
and get their hip replaced.

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.860
But we're looking at the
demographics that show

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.540
this very basic math
that shows baby boomers

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.400
are going to need hips and knees.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:36.420
For every one pound you gain.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.220
it's four pounds on your knees.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:41.590
So while again, there may
not be surgeries right now,

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:43.940
over time, not only will it recover,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.370
but we say that's going to unfortunately

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.100
be a growing industry.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:50.560
So that's what we mean about buying things

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.390
when they're out of favor.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.533
- No, it's very compelling.

00:14:54.533 --> 00:14:58.140
I don't know my old job, I
was an analyst at a hedge fund

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.090
and at much more junior
level than Mellody.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.250
But, my boss always used to say,

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.320
when you're buying there's
someone smart selling to you

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:07.180
and when you're selling,
there's someone smart buying

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.200
so what do you think your edge is?

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:11.960
And Mellody's edge and it
sounds like it's very much

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:13.630
aligned with the Warren
Buffett's of the world

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:17.880
is that most people think
relatively short to medium term

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.640
and kinda move with the
herd and move with emotion.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.810
And if you can somehow
separate yourself from that

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.790
and be a little bit contrarian sometimes,

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.710
you might be able to,
time could be your friend.

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.110
- And that speaks for our logo.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:32.550
And we'd say be a lot contrary
and the number of calls

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.020
that I've gotten from everything

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.210
from working class individuals

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.247
who are trying to make ends meet saying,

00:15:38.247 --> 00:15:42.140
"My 401K plan or 403B
plan is getting crushed."

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:44.217
The billionaires that we work for who say,

00:15:44.217 --> 00:15:45.550
"I'm thinking of selling."

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.040
You don't sell into the panic.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.390
Buffett has this quote, "Buy the panic."

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.537
There's another famous
line, "You wanna be greedy

00:15:52.537 --> 00:15:53.947
"when others are fearful

00:15:53.947 --> 00:15:56.170
"and fearful when others are greedy."

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.200
So during the time of fear and panic,

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.220
indiscriminate selling
which we saw in early March.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:04.200
We're gonna be thinking,
where are the opportunities?

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.670
Again, there's that famous line,

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:07.297
I think it's Churchill who said,

00:16:07.297 --> 00:16:09.900
"Don't let a crisis go to waste."

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:11.720
And so, you say during that crisis,

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.750
just from an investment standpoint,

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:14.960
what are the opportunities

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.400
that might just be being rolling away,

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:19.400
where people are just running for cover.

00:16:20.547 --> 00:16:23.540
You should never sell into
that kind of down market.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.760
That's why we have a turtle as a logo.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.380
And we talk about slow and
steady winning the race

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.030
like Aesop's fables,
and don't get the sense

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:32.590
that we mean slow and being like,

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:33.640
we're not doing anything,

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:35.530
we are urgently patient.

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:38.750
Like we're looking for
opportunities at all time.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.530
We take our time, and we're thinking

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.020
of what will this company be over the next

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:44.410
three to five years,

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.400
not the next three to five quarters.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.860
- I like that phrase, urgently patient.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.883
I think I need to be a little
bit more of that myself.

00:16:51.883 --> 00:16:52.716
(laughs)

00:16:52.716 --> 00:16:56.140
I have the urgency, I need
the more of the patience.

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:57.710
- But that's what we really are.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.420
We're urgently patient, we're
looking at it with urgency

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:04.150
but we're taking a long-term
view about what it'll be.

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:04.983
- That's great.

00:17:04.983 --> 00:17:05.880
And we're getting a ton of questions

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:07.750
you could imagine about
the economy itself.

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.070
And those businesses

00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:11.810
that you just talked
about make a ton of sense,

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:14.940
at least even with my
old investment hat on.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:17.850
But a lot of people and we
had Ray Dalio on last week.

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:20.970
And there's kinda contrasting trends here.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.480
We saw the market go down a lot

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:26.260
and then it's kinda gone back
up to a reasonable level.

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:29.380
It seems to me as someone who's not doing

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:31.030
this as my day job anymore,

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:33.780
that the market got reasonably optimistic

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:36.010
maybe once we hit the peak of the crisis

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:37.460
and things like that.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.410
But at the other hand, when
you look at the jobless claims,

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.800
numbers, you think about well,
even if a state does open up,

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.320
will people really go to
restaurants or dentists

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:47.580
or whatever else?

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:48.783
It could be a while.

00:17:50.054 --> 00:17:51.600
Do you think the market has it wrong?

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.670
Do you think there's
something else at play here?

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.393
What's your macro read?

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:00.100
- So we are bottom-up stock pickers

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:02.750
We're looking at companies
just one company at a time,

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.230
what will it be over the long term

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.370
and we like it best when those companies

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:10.540
don't get beaten up and
down by what's going on

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:11.680
from a macro perspective

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.220
but in this environment
that's unavoidable.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:16.880
What we would say is that we
we don't think of the market

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.450
as being right or wrong, it just is.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.130
And so from that perspective,

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.790
our work is to try to be thoughtful around

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:28.010
what is occurring, and the
market what it typically

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.330
does, it anticipates the future.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.440
It looks out into the future,

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:36.360
and has some sense of what
it thinks will happen.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.850
And then that's obviously as a
result of a cluster of people

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.760
buying and selling et cetera.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.270
And there's no question with the recover

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.210
we've seen in such a short period of time

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:46.200
the market is anticipating

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.070
better days ahead for the economy.

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:52.110
Perhaps they're thinking
that recovery will be faster

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.470
than what everyone thought in early March

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:57.410
when shares were being
indiscriminately sold.

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:00.140
And so we would say that is what it is,

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.500
it could be wrong, we might have a W,

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:07.170
where we go down again, we
may have stayed at home orders

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:09.410
again in the fall, we don't know.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:12.770
We have thought more that
it might look more like a U

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:15.590
that we've had this period
through the rest of the year

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:18.440
where you would not expect a
lot out of corporate America

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:22.020
especially in terms of
profitability or earnings.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:26.060
And then you'd start to
see a recovery in 2021,

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:29.540
which to us is like a
nanosecond in investing.

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:32.610
That is the scenario with
which we're working against.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:35.090
But we can be wrong and still be right.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.220
Because remember, we're
thinking long term,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:38.600
and we're not making investments.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:42.890
We're not trading based upon
the next three to six months.

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:46.940
It's nice to have the market
recover the way it has.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:48.530
But we also know that

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:51.450
it has been a very, very volatile period,

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:55.360
and these things can turn on a dime.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.020
I think our biggest concern right now

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:00.810
is if we come out, way too
fast, and way too early,

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:03.830
that people, could be in a situation where

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:05.370
they have to lock down again,

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.340
that might be very, very upsetting.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.820
So we will just have to play
it by year and see here.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:13.140
And for us, it's really to take advantage

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.093
of the opportunities as they come.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:17.760
- And these businesses like say,

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:19.870
these are associated
with the dental industry

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:23.040
or hip replacement that when
you are in a stay-in-place

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.320
order, maybe even once we're
out of the stay-in-place order,

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:29.710
and you're allowed to go, but
most people will try not to go

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:31.290
until there's a vaccine or something.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.020
How long, when you're looking at how long

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:35.500
they can be solved with little revenue.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.360
What time horizon are you all looking at?

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:38.193
Is it six months?

00:20:38.193 --> 00:20:40.160
18 months longer?
- We're looking at, yeah.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.330
We were looking at the
next six to 12 months, yes.

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:45.900
And saying if they're
really, really compromised

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.730
during that period, will these businesses

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.410
do they have the balance
sheet that they can be okay?

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:51.940
That's the question we're asking ourselves

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:55.060
and that's not a guess,
for certain industries.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.510
I mean, we're gonna see bankruptcies

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:58.140
come out of this environment.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.680
We've already started to see that,

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:02.810
and there whole sectors that
are really, really taking

00:21:02.810 --> 00:21:04.050
it on the chin.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:07.630
I mean, the airline industry
is going to be compromised,

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:09.710
there's no question about it,
because that's an industry

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:13.990
where capital expenditures
are very, very expensive.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:15.950
You've got big labor issues,

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:19.570
now they're benefiting lower oil prices

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:21.830
but there are a whole bunch
of things that can hit them.

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:23.560
And so that would be an
industry that we'd say,

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:26.210
we just don't know what will happen there

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:29.090
that goes into the too
hard category for us.

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.000
But there'll be a whole
bunch of other areas

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:34.930
where you can again, look
out and say, literally,

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:38.678
I know like, if my crown breaks,
I'm going to the dentist.

00:21:38.678 --> 00:21:40.583
It's just it's not going to be a question.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:43.430
- Yeah, and I know you are bottoms-up.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:44.620
And actually the fund I used to work for

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.060
was very, very similar
on a much smaller scale.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.610
But I am I'm sure you do have a view on,

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:52.190
I mean, you just gave a
little bit of your the macro.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.080
But what from a policy point of view?

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:56.250
If you were the chairman
of the Federal Reserve,

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.743
or if you were the Treasury Secretary.

00:22:00.605 --> 00:22:03.590
What kind of actions you think
need to be taken or not taken

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:05.380
to get us through this?

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:08.470
- Well, I have to say I applaud
what they have done so far.

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:10.960
I was in a board meeting
with someone and they said,

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:13.050
the Federal Reserve
didn't bring a bazooka,

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:14.650
they brought the whole arsenal.

00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:15.980
And that is true.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:20.980
This is a giant contrast to
the '08-'09 financial crisis,

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:22.810
when I remember Henry Paulson,

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:24.850
who was been Treasury Secretary,

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:26.370
going and presenting to Congress

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:30.350
and asking for a stimulus bill
that was 750 billion dollars

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.950
and them initially turning him down.

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:36.390
And he did needed that money
to rescue American banks.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:38.810
And that was one of those daunting moments

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:42.760
the market is literally
cratered on that bad news.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.710
This is just the opposite.

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.630
Congress has an open cheque book,

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:49.480
which has its own repercussions
potentially down the road.

00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:51.960
But today, you wanna be in the business

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.340
of doing too much versus too little

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:57.800
and they're approaching
this in exactly that manner.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:00.270
Now that said, I want this stimulus text

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.270
to get to individuals faster,

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:06.120
because that has been
problematic at certain levels.

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:08.790
And I think the small business,

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:13.640
PPP is also a challenge in
terms of how fast people

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:16.290
are getting money, and is everyone getting

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.720
that equal access to the
opportunity to participate.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:22.100
So those things I think
we've got to get on.

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:24.270
The one thing I'll also say
they did a really good job

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:25.780
with a furlough situation

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.180
that when employees get furloughed,

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.290
they're getting their full compensation,

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:33.120
which incense companies not
to lay those people off.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.470
And so I think that's really, really good.

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:37.530
And I think the unemployment benefits

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:40.460
given the number of people
who had to file claims

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:41.293
at the end of the day,

00:23:41.293 --> 00:23:44.010
I think they did the
right thing there as well.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.430
And especially with putting
on the extra four children

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:47.870
and there was a lot there

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.413
that was I think, very, very thoughtful,

00:23:51.579 --> 00:23:53.170
and the right thing to do.

00:23:53.170 --> 00:23:54.650
I just don't think it's over.

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:58.640
I think we're looking at
more stimulus, not less.

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:00.037
- And there's tons of question here

00:24:00.037 --> 00:24:01.690
but one follow up question to that.

00:24:01.690 --> 00:24:03.200
What do you think are the long-term

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:05.310
implications to your point?

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:06.143
In terms of emergence,

00:24:06.143 --> 00:24:07.590
you can think of very fundamental terms.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:08.830
There is food out there,

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.550
we just need to make
sure people have currency

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:11.410
to buy the food.

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:12.570
Are there shelter?

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:15.000
We need to make sure they have
currency to pay that rent.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.700
But in order to do so,
trillions of dollars

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:20.140
potentially of increasing the debt.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.990
What do you think are the
long-term implications, if any?

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:27.550
- Inflation is likely a
long-term implication.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.520
I mean, when we've talked
to a number of experts

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:32.110
out there in academics,

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:35.360
they're pretty united
in their point of view.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:38.160
When Rome is burning, if
your house is burning down,

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:40.670
you're not thinking about
the rebuild at that point

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:42.380
you're thinking about
putting out the fire.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.190
So right now, we're assume
the put out the fire mode.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:48.640
And then we'll have to
figure out the environment

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:50.930
afterwards and what we do about that.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:53.750
But again, I think
there is no other action

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:56.300
that could be taken than the actions

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:58.013
that have been taken thus far.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:24:59.733
- Yeah.

00:24:59.733 --> 00:25:03.230
We have this question from
Tamika Rogers on YouTube.

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:06.160
And you've touched on this
already, but I'll ask it again,

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:07.760
'cause I'm sure a lot of
people are asking this.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:09.760
Miss Hobson, is this the perfect time

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.060
to invest in the stock market?

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:14.350
Well-known companies,
I guess she's asking,

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:15.460
would it be well-known companies,

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:17.320
mostly that you expect to
thrive in two to five years,

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:20.580
which does sound aligned
with y'alls thinking?

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:23.500
- So this is what I
would say, first of all,

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:26.550
I do think there are some
opportunities out there

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:28.710
not as many as there were a month ago

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:31.710
when things were worse.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:33.997
But what I would say, Warren Buffett says,

00:25:33.997 --> 00:25:36.600
"It's a market of stocks,
not a stock market."

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:40.210
So, there are companies always
for sale in the stock market

00:25:40.210 --> 00:25:42.970
even when the stock market is at highs.

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:45.160
I would caution people to make sure

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:46.800
they have the right perspective.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:49.060
And I think you have to
take a longer term view

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:51.600
and not to expect this
to be like a rubber band

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.610
that's recoiled and it just
bounces back like that.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:55.830
So just making sure again,

00:25:55.830 --> 00:26:00.260
you're looking out and you are
patient in your perspective.

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:02.970
I also would encourage you
to invest in things you know.

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:05.560
That's a famous line from
another great investor,

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.580
value investor named Peter Lynch,

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:10.290
who was very famous in the '80s.

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:13.223
And he talked about investing in things

00:26:13.223 --> 00:26:15.390
that you have a good sense of,

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:18.533
as opposed to trying to
stretch and learn something

00:26:18.533 --> 00:26:20.310
that you don't know or understand.

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:22.090
There are certain areas we don't invest in

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:25.270
because we do not understand
them and we think we're smart.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:27.000
But we tend not to buy commodities

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.780
because you have to predict them.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:30.300
There are a whole host of things,

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:33.490
we don't have a lot of
exposure to technology,

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:35.670
because we think there's
rapid obsolescence,

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:40.350
especially amongst smaller and
mid-size technology companies

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.960
that you can be put out
of business very quickly

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.500
by another competitor
or disrupted very easily

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:49.460
and then your value could erode.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:53.810
It's harder for Google now
or harder for Facebook,

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:56.690
but some of this on the smaller
end that can be challenging.

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.870
So we don't tend to do a lot there,

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:02.140
being very realistic about
what you know and understand.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:06.700
I'm not one for stock tips from
people, anything like that.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:09.090
And if you don't have the
stomach for this, don't do it.

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:12.360
Get a professional, buy mutual funds,

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.590
that you have investment
professionals like us in Ariel

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:16.730
that are actually doing the work

00:27:16.730 --> 00:27:19.030
and worrying about this
on a day-to-day basis.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:21.740
- Actually, just a curious question,

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:23.050
something I always struggled with

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:25.580
when I was in the same field is,

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:28.010
you have a strong thesis,
you've done your homework,

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:29.700
you've made your investment.

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:32.913
And y'all are famously patient about it.

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:35.350
Well my question is, are there some times

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:37.100
that you are unsure about yourself?

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:40.160
How do you draw the line between

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:42.040
doubling down on your bet versus saying

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:44.190
maybe I was just wrong
and getting out of it?

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:45.810
And obviously, there's
parallels to life there

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:48.620
but you're living it every
day in the investment world.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:50.830
- So first, we have
this point that we make

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:52.787
with our investment team and we say,

00:27:52.787 --> 00:27:56.810
"We hold our ideas lightly, not dearly."

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:59.460
So we hold them lightly so
that we have the ability

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:02.060
with new information to change our minds.

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:04.110
It's very important.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.597
biz investing, you don't need,
as again quoting Buffett,

00:28:07.597 --> 00:28:10.990
"A perfect batting
average to be successful."

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:14.070
And so as a result of that,
you're going to make mistakes,

00:28:14.070 --> 00:28:15.690
you must accept that.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:17.490
One of the greatest pieces of advice I got

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:20.330
was from my co-CEO and business partner.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.617
Once I was agonizing over
something and he said,

00:28:22.617 --> 00:28:24.967
"Mellody, I don't agonize over mistakes,

00:28:24.967 --> 00:28:27.120
"because I make them frequently."

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:29.080
And you really have to have a little bit

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.010
of a move-on mentality.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:33.020
You make it, what did you learn from that?

00:28:33.020 --> 00:28:34.120
And you move on.

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:37.150
And there are times we have
absolutely made mistakes

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:39.700
we went on internalize those lessons.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:43.400
One of the periods where we
made mistakes was in 2008-2009,

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:46.420
where we underperform for the
first time in a down market,

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.600
and we underperform significantly.

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:52.490
And we went back and completely overhauled

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:55.240
our balance sheet work
and how we were doing it

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:57.080
and making sure companies
have the wherewithal

00:28:57.080 --> 00:28:58.200
for tough periods.

00:28:58.200 --> 00:29:00.730
And we think that those
learnings allowed us

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:03.400
to survive this period
and endure this period

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:06.560
much better than we did
before as an example.

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:09.560
And so we make mistakes
with stocks all the time.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:12.360
And the simple question
we ask our analysts,

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:15.040
this is the easy way to think about it.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:18.540
We don't allow our
analysts to say, "Hold."

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.850
Some companies, you have
a buy, a hold or a sell.

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:25.820
We say you're either a buyer
or seller, you must pick.

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:28.200
If you don't wanna buy
it, you wanna sell it.

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:30.483
And in some ways, that's figurative for us

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:32.730
but it's a way of making people

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:34.180
really put a stake in the ground

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:35.470
either like it or you don't,

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:39.200
you can't live in this
no man's land or hold

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:44.200
without showing your hand
of what you really believe.

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:46.130
- That's a fascinating point is it?

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.080
I've always thought about
it but never phrased

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.460
the way you just said,
'cause your point is right.

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:52.710
If you're holding something,
you're essentially saying,

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:55.440
if you were buying today,
you'd be willing to buy it.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:56.690
- 'Cause right
- Correct.

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:59.010
- Definition holding
that's a fascinating thing,

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:00.260
the rest of the industry (laughs)

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:02.720
I think a lot of the
folks are seeking shelter

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.090
in that hold position (laughs)

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:04.923
'cause you can't be two, right?

00:30:04.923 --> 00:30:08.500
- And the thing is, we have
to affirm that every day.

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:11.650
That's the thing, you
can't rationalize that

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:14.990
because you've owned it for
this whatever amount of time.

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:16.467
Again, quoting a great investor,

00:30:16.467 --> 00:30:20.040
"The stock doesn't know you
own it, it doesn't care."

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.350
So every day, you're
starting off with the idea

00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:25.200
that is this better than
owning something else.

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.683
It doesn't matter how
long I owned it before.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:30.650
- And I really love that idea

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:33.440
of not holding your ideas too tightly.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.510
I think there's a very
big meta wisdom to that.

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:39.120
Even in life our egos
can get very attached

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.860
to our points of views or ideas.

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:44.160
And sometimes even become
destructive (laughs)

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:46.390
our lives 'cause we
hold so tightly to them

00:30:46.390 --> 00:30:47.650
in the face of new information.

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:49.890
So there's something very zen about that.

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:51.630
- Think about that, from the perspective

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:54.250
just politics today and
the polarization of ideas

00:30:54.250 --> 00:30:57.520
around political parties, et cetera.

00:30:57.520 --> 00:31:01.310
And when people become
too wedded to an ideology,

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:05.140
it can break down, real progress.

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:07.800
And so we really do have this perspective

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.630
of that's one of the reasons
we believe diverse points

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:14.270
of view are so important to
solving really hard problems.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:17.650
And bringing people with all
of those diverse points of view

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:20.340
that might have a point of
view that's very strong.

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:23.940
But again, that I'm willing
to yield my perspective

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:26.040
to a more compelling point of view,

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:30.093
as opposed to just being
wedded to an ideology.

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:35.260
- Yeah, no, I mean, that's so
relevant on so many levels.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:38.250
There's a question here
about finance in general

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:41.653
or learning finance from
Aditya Gupta from YouTube.

00:31:44.379 --> 00:31:45.950
How to start learning
about finance investing

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:47.920
in the economy at a younger age?

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:50.373
How can we start investing at our ages?

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:53.690
- So first and foremost,
this is one of the things

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:57.700
that just you know, Sal, I
came to see you on this topic

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:00.900
because the thing that
frustrates me so much is that

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:02.300
I'm just gonna use America

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:04.690
as the base for the conversation here.

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:06.570
You can go to high school in America today

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:10.440
and take woodshop or auto
and not a class on investing,

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:12.740
which always leads me to
ask audiences the same thing

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:15.090
who's wiggling in their spare time?

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:16.880
Who's cleaning their own carburetor?

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:18.390
No one, right?

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:19.670
But when it comes to understanding

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.980
the basics of the Dow,
the S&amp;P, the NASDAQ,

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.840
small cap, mid cap, large
cap, market capitalization,

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:27.850
growth, value, all of these things

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:32.640
that have some profound potential effect

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:37.640
on your long-term financial
security, we don't teach it.

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:40.980
And so that's why opportunities
like this are so important.

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:43.300
What I believe very
strongly is that investing

00:32:43.300 --> 00:32:47.180
should be taught in school in
America, at a very young age.

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:49.510
I think it should be
like learning a language.

00:32:49.510 --> 00:32:52.300
I have a six-year old and
she takes Spanish at school,

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:54.230
she already speaks Chinese.

00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:57.160
And once you can start
early, you become very facile

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:00.690
in the language and I think
that's very, very important.

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:02.920
One way if you can't get it in a school

00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:04.950
to get them to have it
as part of the curriculum

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:06.140
and I say to people,

00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:09.650
if you have a stockbroker
or financial advisor,

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:11.150
the person in the local bank

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:12.860
that may know something about investments,

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:14.940
maybe they can come in and teach a class

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:17.590
for your nephew or your
child or what have you,

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:21.080
so you get a sense of
these important concepts.

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:23.510
The children get a sense of
these important concepts.

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:26.560
If you can't do that, I
think a great idea is to do

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:30.140
what John Rogers, my business
partner's father did for him.

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:33.100
His father was a child of
The Depression, an orphan.

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:35.910
He moved to Chicago,
when he was 11 years old.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:38.420
He was in the first
class of Tuskegee Airmen

00:33:38.420 --> 00:33:40.730
and he wanted John to understand money.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:42.170
And so he gave John stocks

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:43.780
every birthday and every Christmas

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:47.130
starting when he was 12
years old, instead of toys.

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:49.760
That you may not do the
instead of toys part,

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:51.770
but certainly having that component,

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:55.160
be a part of his gift-giving
really taught John

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.920
from the very beginning the
fundamentals of investing,

00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:00.130
and you can do that with
a child by giving him

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:03.050
gifts of stock of things
that they know and like.

00:34:03.050 --> 00:34:06.250
And that's the gift that
keeps giving versus the toy

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:08.610
that becomes obsolete at some point.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:10.110
And that could be Mattel,

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:13.010
that could be shares of
Sony because of PlayStation,

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:15.260
that could be McDonald's or Nike,

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:17.610
things that the children
understand fundamentally

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:21.440
or your teen, and ultimately
may create some interest

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:23.600
there that they can watch and understand

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:26.560
the benefits of ownership and equity,

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:29.453
as opposed to just consumerism.

00:34:30.700 --> 00:34:32.950
- Yeah, that's powerful idea.

00:34:32.950 --> 00:34:35.260
And I actually think,
it's talking about edges

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:36.560
that I think some younger people

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:37.840
because they think a little bit different

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:39.220
have a different lens,
they actually might be able

00:34:39.220 --> 00:34:41.780
to have a perspective that professionals

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:44.540
might not even fully have.

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:47.983
There's a question here from Layla Torres.

00:34:47.983 --> 00:34:50.307
It's really praise, but I
think it leads to a question,

00:34:50.307 --> 00:34:51.355
"Mellody, thanks for being a role model

00:34:51.355 --> 00:34:53.767
"to all females in finance.

00:34:53.767 --> 00:34:54.707
"We don't have many

00:34:54.707 --> 00:34:56.240
"and it was great to learn about you."

00:34:56.240 --> 00:34:57.520
And the question I'll ask is,

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:59.863
why don't we see more females in finance?

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:03.520
- I think there's a lot
of stigma around issues

00:35:03.520 --> 00:35:05.370
related to math and girls.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:08.620
And, I'm not the first
person who's ever said this.

00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:12.500
And it's disappointing,
and it continues to exist.

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:13.350
And it's true.

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:16.500
And that, therefore, affects our ranks

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.410
in both the investment business

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:21.340
as well as in engineering and other areas.

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:24.030
And I think it's a
perception that's just wrong,

00:35:24.030 --> 00:35:25.780
the data shows it.

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:28.870
So we've looked at the
investment habits, patterns

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:31.850
and successes of women versus men.

00:35:31.850 --> 00:35:35.920
And interestingly, women
tend to be better investors.

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:38.720
Part of the reason that we
tend to be better ambassadors,

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:41.760
we tend to trade less and be more patient.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:43.810
And that ultimately works in our benefit.

00:35:43.810 --> 00:35:46.410
And they've looked at
lots and lots of decisions

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:49.230
that have been made over
time to be able to show that

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:51.020
that's just rank and file people

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:53.400
in their personal investment portfolios.

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:54.650
In the industry that I'm in,

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:58.210
only 7% of portfolio managers are women.

00:35:58.210 --> 00:36:00.870
We actually have one in my
office that's great investor

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.360
named Rupal Bhansali,

00:36:02.360 --> 00:36:04.570
who manages our international
global strategies

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:06.930
and has done a phenomenal job.

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:10.450
But it's really disappointing
to see those numbers

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:12.150
haven't moved very much.

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:14.090
Now I think exposure is part of that

00:36:14.090 --> 00:36:17.150
they need to see people like
Rupal and people like me,

00:36:17.150 --> 00:36:19.940
who love what we do,
and are in this business

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:21.190
and in it to win it.

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:24.580
And I think to the extent
that girls and teens

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:27.210
and college students are
aware of the opportunities

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:28.790
in our industry and the fulfillment

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:30.780
that we get from the work that we do,

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.380
I think they would be as
excited about it as we are.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:35.502
- And related to that.

00:36:35.502 --> 00:36:38.680
Another comment this is
from Rushika Tellim Saidi

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:40.947
from YouTube said, "Mellody
Hobson, you are so inspiring

00:36:40.947 --> 00:36:42.227
"to all girls around the world.

00:36:42.227 --> 00:36:44.590
"I'm a girl of color and
I'm really inspired by you."

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:47.283
And that kind of going
with a question from Layla.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:51.320
When you went into the
industry right out of college,

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:54.450
and you looked around at
probably were even fewer women,

00:36:54.450 --> 00:36:58.927
even fewer people of color,
did you ever feel like,

00:36:58.927 --> 00:37:02.010
"Hey, how come there's no one
like me, maybe I don't belong"

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:05.140
and how did you overcome
any feelings like that?

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:08.160
- So I'm smiling because
I had a friend once

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:10.910
that I asked a question
about being different

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:12.150
in the industry, et cetera.

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.747
And he was like "Mellody,
how long have you been black?

00:37:14.747 --> 00:37:16.770
"How long have you been a woman?"

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:18.300
It just comes with the territory,

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:20.200
when you're a woman or a person of color,

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:22.430
you'll understand what you're up against.

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:24.330
I've told stories about how
when I was a young girl,

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:26.980
my mother told me what
I would be up against.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:29.670
And she explained to me,
I have it in my TED Talk,

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:31.290
that people won't always treat me well.

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:33.550
She told me that when I was six years old.

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:35.430
And it was really something
that was very important

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:38.000
because it allowed me
not to then be defeated

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.610
by any form of inequity that I saw.

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:42.180
I saw the opportunity when I entered

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:44.860
the industry in 1991, to be different.

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:46.010
And so I joke with people

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:47.640
when I would go to investment conferences,

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:50.710
I would be the only one,
the only woman of color.

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:53.450
And when I go up to people,
they'd say, "You're Mellody"

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:54.840
before they had even met me.

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:57.480
And I could have been like Cher or Beyonce

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:00.870
like no last name, I stood out.

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:03.550
And so I used that standing
out to my advantage.

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:05.147
I said, "Okay, there's
no one else like me.

00:38:05.147 --> 00:38:07.160
"That I'm going to be memorable."

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:09.990
As opposed to seeing it as a cross

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:11.680
that I was carrying on my back.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:13.410
It doesn't mean that it was always easy

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.910
by any stretch of the imagination.

00:38:15.910 --> 00:38:19.360
The way that I built up my
confidence is through knowledge.

00:38:19.360 --> 00:38:23.210
The more knowledge I have,
the more prepared I am,

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:24.830
the more confident I am.

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:26.480
And I think that's really important.

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:28.310
We all know what it takes for us to walk

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:30.240
into a room and feel confident.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:32.680
If I was studied in my subject

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:35.360
I could walk in with my head high

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.920
and with my ideas ready to go.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:40.970
And I think that's a very, very important

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:44.290
way of conquering the world.

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:45.820
When you can go on with good ideas

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.680
and not be afraid to state what they are

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:49.730
not be discouraged when they're rejected.

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:52.690
I always felt like I could get another job

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:53.523
was the other thing.

00:38:53.523 --> 00:38:54.947
I was like, "If it doesn't work out here,

00:38:54.947 --> 00:38:57.430
"I'm really confident in myself."

00:38:57.430 --> 00:38:59.840
Not because I'm cocky or have a huge ego.

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:02.240
I just know I'll do whatever it takes.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:05.010
If tomorrow you told me I
couldn't go work at Ariel

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:07.180
and I had to go and work at Neiman Marcus

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:11.120
or Macy's or Kohl's or you name the store,

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:14.100
I'd be the number one
salesperson without a doubt,

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.380
because I would do it with
that much as much passion

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.440
as I do this proudly.

00:39:18.440 --> 00:39:21.350
So I really think it's
about your own mindset

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:23.040
and your own mentality.

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:24.770
There are going to be haters.

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:27.770
They exist everywhere, and big and small

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:30.370
and your own race and your own gender.

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:32.970
And they're gonna be people
who root for you and help you.

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:36.210
I just I decided to ignore
those who weren't there

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:39.310
to help me, try to avoid
them as much as possible,

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:41.160
make choices that would lead me to people

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:43.710
who are more positive and more interested

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:45.650
in a better outcome for me.

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:48.530
And that doesn't mean I got it perfectly.

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:50.810
But more often than not by giving people

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:52.530
the benefit of the doubt,

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:54.053
I was more right than wrong.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.320
- Wow that's an incredibly inspiring

00:39:57.320 --> 00:40:01.870
and I think everyone has
aspects about their being

00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:03.950
that they might feel a little
bit different than others

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:06.600
or feel imposter or feel
insecure about certain things.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:08.180
But I think the way
you framed it right now

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:11.150
is inspiring for everyone.

00:40:11.150 --> 00:40:13.450
Mellody, I could keep going for hours

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:15.960
asking you questions and it looks like

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:17.950
YouTube and Facebook could
keep going on, as well.

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:20.040
But I wanna be very
sensitive to your time.

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.630
Thank you so much for for joining this.

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:23.530
And I hope you join again.

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:24.490
Hopefully, you had a good time.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.830
There's a lot of questions
from a lot of folks here.

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:29.210
But this was a really
inspiring conversation.

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:30.840
Thank you so much.

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:31.970
- Well, thank you for having me.

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:34.170
And I just really wanna
end by thanking you

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:37.090
for all the work you do, Sal for so many.

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:41.660
You are one of my heroes, I
mean that genuinely in my heart

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:43.870
and I'm just so proud to know you

00:40:43.870 --> 00:40:46.090
and to be able to learn from you.

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:47.260
- Well, you're one of my heroes,

00:40:47.260 --> 00:40:50.400
and it's only been reinforced
from this conversation.

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:53.170
So I'm extra flattered
that you even say that

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:54.653
but thank you so much Mellody,

00:40:54.653 --> 00:40:56.340
it's a real treat.
- Thanks.

00:40:57.550 --> 00:41:00.510
- So everyone, thanks
for joining what I found

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:04.400
to be a very inspiring
conversation with Mellody.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:05.700
A lot of takeaways that
I'm gonna be thinking

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:09.130
about as I have my own.

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:10.870
I think we all have our own things

00:41:10.870 --> 00:41:12.270
that we deal with in life.

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:15.500
I just loved Mellody's
ability to power through

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:16.910
and be resilient and optimistic

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:20.350
and in the face of sometimes
significant adversity.

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:23.250
But anyway thanks for joining,
I hope we can have Mellody

00:41:23.250 --> 00:41:24.570
on in the future as well.

00:41:24.570 --> 00:41:26.210
We're gonna have some other amazing guests

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:28.550
in the next few days and weeks,

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:33.483
and stay safe, stay health
and these times of COVID.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:37.490
And just remind yourself that
we're all in this together.

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:39.610
Don't try to focus too much
on some of the negatives

00:41:39.610 --> 00:41:40.443
that you hear in the news.

00:41:40.443 --> 00:41:42.080
There's a lot of positives out there.

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:45.270
And one of the silver
linings is we are seeing

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:48.640
a lot of humanity step up
and do the right thing.

00:41:48.640 --> 00:41:50.180
I'll also just throw out the plug again,

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:51.640
because it's part of my job.

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:52.950
We are not-for-profit.

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.050
If you're in a position to do so,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.380
please think about making
a donation to Khan Academy.

00:41:57.380 --> 00:41:58.320
It's donations like that,

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:00.850
that allow us to continue to do this work,

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:03.350
especially for folks
who might not otherwise

00:42:03.350 --> 00:42:06.970
be able to have access
to learning materials.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:09.723
So thank you so much, and
I will see you tomorrow.

